 ## Subtopics
 
+### [Diffuse ABSDF Factorization](10870-diffuse-absdf-factorization.md)
+A method for approximating the diffuse component of light transport in participating media by separating integration over material parameters from integration over geometry and orientation. It sets itself apart from sibling methods by focusing specifically on the diffuse lobe, allowing the use of Spherical Gaussian approximations for the cosine-weighted normal distribution. Choose it when efficiently evaluating the diffuse scattering term in path tracing is required.
+
 ### [Specular ABSDF Factorization](10872-specular-absdf-factorization.md)
-Specular ABSDF Factorization is a method for computing the aggregated specular response of a surface with varying material parameters (roughness, orientation) by factorizing the integral of the microfacet BRDF over a probability distribution of these parameters. It solves the problem of efficiently evaluating the expectation of a complex microfacet distribution when the roughness $\alpha$ and normal $n$ are not single values but follow a joint probability density function $p_Y(\gamma)$. It sets itself apart from its siblings by explicitly separating the Fresnel mix terms from the microfacet distribution convolution, allowing the shadowing-masking term $G$ to be applied post-convolution due to its smoothness, and supporting orientation-varying parameters via spherical kernel splatting.
+A method that computes the aggregated specular response of a surface with spatially varying material parameters by factorizing the integral of the microfacet BRDF over a joint probability distribution of those parameters. It sets itself apart by explicitly separating Fresnel mix terms from the microfacet distribution convolution and applying the shadowing-masking term post-convolution. Choose it when evaluating the expectation of a complex microfacet distribution under orientation-varying parameters is required.
 
 ### [Phys3DGS BRDF Function](11071-phys3dgs-brdf-function.md)
-A physically-based Bidirectional Reflectance Distribution Function (BRDF) used in 3D Gaussian Splatting for inverse rendering, consisting of a diffuse component and a specular component based on the GGX microfacet model with a correlated Smith masking-shadowing function. It differs from its parent, the Modified Disney Principled BRDF, by employing a specific roughness remapping ($\alpha = r^2$) and a correlated Smith geometry function rather than the separable Schlick-GGX approximation, aiming for higher physical accuracy in the context of Gaussian splatting optimization.
+A physically-based BRDF function used in 3D Gaussian Splatting for inverse rendering, consisting of a diffuse and a specular component. It sets itself apart from its parent by employing a specific roughness remapping ($\alpha = r^2$) and a correlated Smith masking-shadowing function rather than a separable approximation. Choose it when higher physical accuracy is needed for Gaussian splatting optimization.
 
 ### [Isotropic Disney BRDF](14196-isotropic-disney-brdf.md)
-The Isotropic Disney BRDF is a simplified material model based on the Cook-Torrance microfacet framework, designed to model reflectance for both metals and plastics in a unified manner. It is a restricted subset of the full Disney Principled BRDF, optimized for differentiable rendering and optimization tasks where ambiguity from high-dimensional parameter spaces must be reduced. Unlike the full model, it restricts optimization to opaque surfaces with spatially varying diffuse albedo and global constants for roughness, specular albedo, and metalness.
-
-### Diffuse ABSDF Factorization
-A sub-component of the ABSDF framework that handles the analytical integration of the diffuse term. It assumes $\beta_c$ and $\beta_m$ are orientation-independent, allowing the joint parameter PDF to split into marginal PDFs. The integral $\int_{[0,1]^2} (1 - \beta_m) \beta_c p(\beta_c, \beta_m) d\beta_c d\beta_m$ simplifies to $E[\beta_c] - E[\beta_m \beta_c]$ [S1, unofficial]. For orientation-varying parameters, the clamped dot product function $\langle n \cdot \omega_i \rangle \langle n \cdot \omega_o \rangle$ is approximated by a Spherical Gaussian (SG) $c \cdot SG(\omega_h; n, \kappa)$, reducing the problem to an SG-convolution [S1, unofficial].
+A simplified material model based on the Cook–Torrance microfacet framework, designed to model reflectance for both metals and plastics in a unified framework. It sets itself apart by restricting optimization to opaque surfaces with spatially varying diffuse albedo and global constants for roughness, specular albedo, and metalness. Choose it for differentiable rendering and optimization tasks where ambiguity from high-dimensional parameter spaces must be reduced.
 
